## How this app is deployed and graded

This section is added by the harness, identically for every task. It describes
how your work is collected and run; the brief above describes what to build.

**Write `/app/Dockerfile`.** Grading does not reuse this container. It runs
`docker build /app` against your source alone, then starts the resulting image as
a container of its own on the same network as the backing services. Nothing from
this session comes with it: no running processes, no packages you installed
outside `/app`, no caches you warmed, no rows you created by hand. The database
is re-created from scratch, so your schema, migrations and seed must be applied
by the image itself.

The container must serve the app in the foreground on the port this brief names,
bound to `0.0.0.0`, and read every service address from the environment at
container start — no service is reachable during `docker build`.

**Check whether you have a Docker daemon to test with.** Run `docker info`. If it
answers, a daemon has been reserved for you and you should not finish until you
have built your image and confirmed the health endpoint answers from it. If the
command is missing or cannot connect, this task has no daemon: write the
`Dockerfile` carefully and read it back as if you were the machine executing it,
step by step, asking at each one what is present in the image at that moment.

If the daemon IS available, two facts about it you cannot discover on your own,
and which will otherwise cost you time:

- A container you start with `--network host` is reachable at **`http://dockerd:<port>`**,
  not `http://localhost:<port>`. `--network host` means the daemon's network, not
  this container's. Your app has not failed just because `localhost` refuses.
- Inside it, the backing services resolve by the same hostnames they use here, so
  a real database connection is part of the test. Pass the environment variables
  your app needs with `-e`, as grading will.

The daemon is yours alone and shares nothing with the grader, so images you build
here are invisible to it. **Grading rebuilds from your source**, which means a
working container proves nothing by itself — what is graded is the `Dockerfile`.
If you fix something by hand inside a running container, put the fix in the
`Dockerfile`.
